{"id":"spa-t0-0001","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.morning","domain":1,"text_native":"Buenos días","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Good morning.","source_caption":"Good morning","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Used until roughly midday. Getting the time of day wrong is noticed; not saying it is only noticed by someone expecting it.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Spanish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Spanish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"spa-t0-0002","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.afternoon","domain":1,"text_native":"tarde","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Good afternoon.","source_caption":"afternoon","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The middle of the day. In several languages this is the same word as the evening greeting, and using it late is not an error.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Spanish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Spanish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"spa-t0-0003","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.evening","domain":1,"text_native":"Buenas noches","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Good evening.","source_caption":"Good evening / Good night","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"From late afternoon. Often interchangeable with the afternoon greeting.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Spanish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Spanish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"spa-t0-0004","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.how-are-you","domain":1,"text_native":"¿Cómo estás?","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"How are you?","source_caption":"How are you?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The opening move in almost every conversation. In most of these languages it is a greeting rather than a question and does not expect a literal answer.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Spanish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Spanish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"spa-t0-0005","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.i-am-well","domain":1,"text_native":"Muy bien, gracias.","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"I am well, thank you.","source_caption":"Fine, thank you","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The expected answer to the greeting above. Answering it wrong reads as evasive in cultures where it is a formula.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Spanish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Spanish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"spa-t0-0006","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.nice-to-meet-you","domain":1,"text_native":"Encantado/a","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Nice to meet you.","source_caption":"Nice to meet you","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Said on a first introduction. In several languages there is a separate phrase for meeting someone formally that a stranger will use instead.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Spanish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Spanish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"spa-t0-0007","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.what-is-your-name","domain":1,"text_native":"¿Cómo te llamas?","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"What is your name?","source_caption":"What is your name?","register":"neutral","direction":"understand","why":"You get asked this before you get to ask it. Recognising the question is the difference between answering and freezing.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Spanish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Spanish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"spa-t0-0008","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.please","domain":1,"text_native":"Por favor","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Please.","source_caption":"Please","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Not a filler in English, and a grammatical particle in some of these languages — one word that changes a sentence from an order to a request.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Spanish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Spanish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"spa-t0-0009","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.thank-you","domain":1,"text_native":"Gracias","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Thank you.","source_caption":"Thank you","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The single most useful word in any of these languages. Saying it costs nothing and changes how everything after it is received.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Spanish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Spanish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"spa-t0-0010","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.youre-welcome","domain":1,"text_native":"De nada","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"You are welcome.","source_caption":"You're welcome","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"You will be handed it constantly, and there is no way to avoid replying. In a few languages the reply is the same word as 'yes'.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Spanish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Spanish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"spa-t0-0011","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.excuse-me","domain":1,"text_native":"Disculpe","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Excuse me.","source_caption":"Excuse me (getting attention)","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Two different things in one phrase: getting someone's attention, and getting past them. Guessing wrong is jarring — one version walks through a conversation.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Spanish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Spanish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"spa-t0-0012","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.sorry","domain":1,"text_native":"Lo siento","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"I am sorry.","source_caption":"I'm sorry","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Apologising for a small fault — bumping someone, being late, not understanding. The form you use signals how much of a fault you think it was.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Spanish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Spanish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"spa-t0-0013","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.goodbye","domain":1,"text_native":"Adiós","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Goodbye.","source_caption":"Goodbye","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"How you leave. Leaving without one is noticeably rude in most of these languages.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Spanish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Spanish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"spa-t0-0014","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.take-care","domain":1,"text_native":"Que pase/pases un buen día","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Take care.","source_caption":"Have a good day","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The longer goodbye, and the one that implies you may meet again. Usually follows the first goodbye rather than replacing it.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Spanish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Spanish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"spa-t0-0015","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"repair.dont-understand","domain":12,"text_native":"No entiendo","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"I do not understand.","source_caption":"I don't understand","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The one phrase that keeps a conversation alive. Without it people stop trying, and with it they often rephrase in simpler words.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Spanish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Spanish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"spa-t0-0016","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"repair.speak-slower","domain":12,"text_native":"¿Podría usted hablar más despacio por favor?","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Please speak more slowly.","source_caption":"Could you speak more slowly please?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Asks for the thing you actually need. Saying it is nearly always received well, even badly pronounced, and asking again is not.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Spanish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Spanish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"spa-t0-0017","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"repair.cannot-hear","domain":12,"text_native":"¿Podría usted repetirlo por favor?","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"I cannot hear you.","source_caption":"Could you repeat that please?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Distinguishes not hearing from not understanding, because they have different fixes and conflating them produces the wrong one.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Spanish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Spanish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"spa-t0-0018","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"repair.how-do-you-say","domain":12,"text_native":"¿Cómo se dice buenas tardes en alemán?","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"How do you say this?","source_caption":"How do you say \"good afternoon\" in German?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"How you learn a word instead of nodding. Asking is how a phrasebook gets used instead of recited.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"attested","id":"Tatoeba spa-en sentence pairs (CC BY 2.0 FR)","url":"https://tatoeba.org/en/downloads","licence":"CC BY 2.0 FR, some CC0","external_id":"1563585","author":"haomakk"},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"spa-t0-0019","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"repair.help-me","domain":12,"text_native":"¿Puede usted ayudarme por favor?","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Can you help me, please?","source_caption":"Can you help me please?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The opener for every request that follows. Standing in front of someone not being asked anything is how people get ignored.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Spanish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Spanish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"spa-t0-0020","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"bridge.speak-english","domain":12,"text_native":"¿Hablas inglés?","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Do you speak English?","source_caption":"Do you speak English?","register":"neutral","direction":"understand","why":"Asked of you constantly, and the question that decides whether anything after it can happen. There is a polite version and a blunt version and the gap between them is wide.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Spanish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Spanish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"spa-t0-0021","lang":"spa","tier":0,"slot":"bridge.i-understand","domain":12,"text_native":"Entiendo.","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"I understand.","source_caption":"I understand.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Saying it when you do not is worse than silence, because it removes the reason to simplify. Said when you do, it invites the harder version.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Spanish phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Spanish_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
